In Unit 2 so far we learnt how to find derivatives. This section shows the first big use of a derivative: the value \(f'(x_0)\) is the slope of the curve at the point where \(x = x_0\). Once we know a point and a slope, the point-slope form of a line (Class XI) gives the line's equation. So every tangent or normal question has the same plan:
1. find the point \((x_0, y_0)\) on the curve;
2. find \(\frac{dy}{dx}\) and put \(x = x_0\) to get the slope \(m\);
3. write the line in point-slope form.
